WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:04.970
When The Incredibles hit theaters in 2004,
it was obvious that it was destined to become

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:05.600
a classic.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.900
The clever spin on a superhero family was
a massive critical success that earned two

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.260
Academy Awards and raked in well over half
a billion dollars at the box office.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.600
You can chalk that up to a thrilling storyline,
fantastic animation, and great performances,

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:23.569
but it also has a lot to do with the fact
that The Incredibles is one of the smartest

00:00:23.569 --> 00:00:26.859
and most layered takes on superheroes to ever
hit the screen.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.060
If you haven’t watched it since you were
a kid, here are a few of the things you’ll

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.280
only notice as an adult.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.620
The jokes for the grown-ups

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.400
Like a lot of films directed primarily at
kids, Pixar movies usually contain a little

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.270
bit of humor for the parents in the audience.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.620
Sometimes, it’s the kind of obvious gag
that might lead to having "The Talk" a little

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.880
earlier than they expected...

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.920
… and of course, there are the scenes of
a very happy Helen Parr pulling her husband

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:50.600
back to bed.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.340
Others come so fast that if you blink, you
might miss them.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.880
In the early montage about how supers have
fallen from grace, for example, one newspaper

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.850
headline shows that a hero with X-ray vision
was using his powers to become a high-tech

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:02.660
peeping tom.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.000
No wonder they got rid of all those super-creeps.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.900
The unsung genius

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.610
Edna Mode, The Incredibles' resident fashionista
and costume designer, is a huge part of the

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.300
film, but her costumes aren’t the only super-tech
that’s used by the heroes.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:21.560
In just the first few minutes, Mr. Incredible
demonstrates his car's tricked out GPS system,

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.080
auto-drive function, and its ability to transform
into a much speedier vehicle upon request.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.780
And that’s just one car!

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:32.240
The thing is, they never once mention the
person who created it all, giving the Incredibles

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.860
universe its most mysterious unsung hero.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.640
How did they get so many wonderful toys that
the rest of the world doesn’t have access

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.360
to, and what happened to them after the supers
went underground?

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.860
Syndrome has the tech, but he doesn’t seem
like the type to share, and no offense, but

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.400
there’s no way that Bob built his own flying
car.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.320
Somebody out there deserves a little more
recognition.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.420
Edna’s voice

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.240
With her snappy dialogue and pitch-perfect
delivery, it’s not surprising that Edna

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.980
Mode was The Incredibles' breakout character.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:01.880
“No capes!”

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.780
The surprise only comes later, when you find
out that the actor behind the diminutive fashionista

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.360
was none other than writer/director Brad Bird.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.880
Bird recorded the character's vocals while
making temporary voice tracks for the film,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.960
and even he approached actresses like Lily
Tomlin about the part.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.580
However, he said that the other employees
at Pixar liked his vocals enough that eventually,

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.360
they stuck.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.900
Bird isn't the only unlikely voice actor to
wind up in the film, though.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.360
Kari the Parr's babysitter, is played by animator
Bret Parker, production designer Lou Romano

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.180
lent his voice to Dash's teacher, and Bird's
son Nick voiced the Parr’s shocked neighbor.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.180
“That was totally wicked!”

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.410
Elastigirl is definitely a feminist

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:46.660
If we’re all being honest, the real hero
of The Incredibles is Elastigirl.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.430
She’s a good mother, a supportive wife,
and the half of her marriage that doesn’t

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.540
get duped into a secret supervillain plot.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.400
Her iconic introduction tells us exactly why:

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.740
“Leave saving the world to the men?

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:58.860
I don’t think so!”

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.850
Holly Hunter, who voices Elastigirl, has credited
the character's outspoken persona to writer/director

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.800
Brad Bird, calling him a "true feminist" in
a recent interview, and that shows up in the

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:09.300
film.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.640
She’s not afraid to step back into action
when she's needed, standing up as a strong

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.900
feminist role model.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.120
Kari deserves a raise

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.840
Most kids watching The Incredibles for the
first time are probably a bit too young to

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:25.930
have taken on their first babysitting job,
but once you have, it's easy to see the whole

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:30.360
thing as a horror story starring Kari, the
Parrs’ poor, put-upon babysitter.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:37.960
“Jack-Jack is fine but there's some really
weird things happening ...Jack Jack is still fine but I’m gettin’

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.060
really weirded out!”

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.460
This fidgety teen is asked to go well above
and beyond the call of duty in her time taking

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.960
care of the young Jack-Jack after the Parrs
left him behind on their mission to save the

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.460
world.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.620
We’ve looked, and they never actually covered
spontaneous combustion in any of the Babysitter’s

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.200
Club books.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.640
Kari may get a bit of a bad rap for giving
Jack-Jack up to Syndrome when he finally relieves

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.349
her after a stressful night, but for five
bucks an hour, you’d probably hand that

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:02.560
kid off to an adult, too.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.150
At the end of the day, Kari tried her best,
and she deserves to be fully compensated for

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.820
it and get a great letter of recommendation
from Bob and Helen as well.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.460
It skewers superhero tropes

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.459
While the Incredibles have only ever been
featured in the one big story that we see

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:22.130
on the screen, they feel familiar too, and
not just because they argue like real families

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.130
on road trips.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:23.700
"Great.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:24.220
We missed it.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.000
You asked me how to get there and I told you.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:26.620
Take a left.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.580
That'll take me downtown.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.740
Get in the right lane.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.700
YELLING!"

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.540
They also have a lot of similarities to some
very famous superheroes, particularly the

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.580
Fantastic Four.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.640
“Mr. Incredible” obviously echoes the
FF’s Mister Fantastic, - in name at least

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.740
- and a quartet featuring a big strong guy,
a stretchy hero, and an invisible girl with

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.780
force fields might seem pretty familiar to
Marvel fans, too.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.199
Even though Dash is the odd one out in that
comparison, his super-speed and red costume

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.360
are consciously reminiscent of the Flash.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.700
It’s not just the major characters, either:
The quick snippets of other superheroes shown

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:06.430
in the film also seem inspired by the classics,
including Frozone's ice powers and Gazerbeam's

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.280
Cyclops-esque laser eyes.

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:12.820
Of course, while it’s obvious that the creators
of The Incredibles love the superhero genre,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.120
they’re not above poking fun at its more
ludicrous extremes, either.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.720
All those lines about villainous monologuing
are a pretty clear shot at old-school comic

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.480
book dialogue.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.640
“You sly dog.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.060
You got me monologuing!”

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:32.860
Even more important: Edna Mode’s entire
existence is based around one of the biggest

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.800
plot holes in the history of comics.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.180
The stylist was born after Brad Bird started
to wonder how every superhero suddenly became

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.080
an expert with the needle and thread after
getting their powers and realized that somebody

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.919
else had to be designing these flamboyant
costumes.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.840
"You're the best of the best!"

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.340
"I know darling."

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:50.800
Thanks for watching!

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.000
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

